o himself penned no works. Clitomachus bridged the intellectual traditions of the Hellenistic world, carrying Carthaginian roots to the philosophical heart of Athens and influencing subsequent Roman thought.

After his early education in Carthage, Clitomachus moved to Athens around 163 BCE, where he became a devoted student of Carneades, the founder of the Third or New Academy. He diligently recorded and systematized Carneades' complex arguments, particularly the principle of *epochē* (suspension of judgment) and the development of *pithanon* (the probable) as a guide for practical action in the absence of absolute certainty. Clitomachus authored over 400 books, though nearly all are lost, preserving Carneades' thought against dogmatic Stoic and Epicurean assertions.

Clitomachus's meticulous documentation ensured the survival and dissemination of Carneades' highly influential Sceptical arguments, which profoundly impacted later Hellenistic and Roman philosophy. His works, though fragmented, were a primary source for Roman intellectuals like Cicero, who relied on Clitomachus to articulate the Academic Sceptic position in his own philosophical dialogues, thereby shaping the intellectual landscape of the late Roman Republic. The transmission of these Sceptical methods contributed to a broader critical tradition within Western thought, questioning the foundations of knowledge."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:02:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40168899637", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "186 BCE, Carthage"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "186 BCE, Carthage"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual ferment of Hellenistic Athens provided a fertile ground for diverse philosophical schools, including Academic Scepticism.", "Carneades' decision to write nothing necessitated a diligent student to record and transmit his complex philosophical arguments.", "The cultural exchange between Carthage and Athens fostered intellectual migration, allowing figures like Clitomachus to contribute to Greek philosophy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:02:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40168899637", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "109 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "109 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Clitomachus's extensive writings preserved and disseminated the radical Sceptical doctrines of Carneades for 